##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   7128 1451  185
                                                                  7128 1451  186
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 7128 1451  187
                                                                  7128 1451  188
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     7128 1451  189
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   7128 1451  190
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             7128 1451  191
                                                                  7128 1451  192
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                7128 1451  193
                                                                  7128 1451  194
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       7128 1451  195
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  7128 1451  196
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   7128 1451  197
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  7128 1451  198
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  7128 1451  199
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 7128 1451  200
to the highest incident energy.                                   7128 1451  201
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:7128 1451  202
                                                                  7128 1451  203
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  7128 1451  204
                                                                  7128 1451  205
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    7128 1451  206
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     7128 1451  207
                                                                  7128 1451  208
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     7128 1451  209
                                                                  7128 1451  210
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    7128 1451  211
total cross section.                                              7128 1451  212
                                                                  7128 1451  213
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            7128 1451  214
                                                                  7128 1451  215
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    7128 1451  216
                                                                  7128 1451  217
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        7128 1451  218
                                                                  7128 1451  219
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  7128 1451  220
                                                                  7128 1451  221
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           7128 1451  222
                                                                  7128 1451  223
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   7128 1451  224
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        7128 1451  225
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     7128 1451  226
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         7128 1451  227
sections.                                                         7128 1451  228
                                                                  7128 1451  229
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 7128 1451  230
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 7128 1451  231
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 7128 1451  232
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 7128 1451  233
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      7128 1451  234
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       7128 1451  235
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              7128 1451  236
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  7128 1451  237
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  7128 1451  238
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  7128 1451  239
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  7128 1451  240
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  7128 1451  241
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       7128 1451  242
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        7128 1451  243
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        7128 1451  244
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        7128 1451  245
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        7128 1451  246
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        7128 1451  247
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        7128 1451  248
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        7128 1451  249
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       7128 1451  250
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        7128 1451  251
                                                                  7128 1451  252
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           7128 1451  253
                                                                  7128 1451  254
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    7128 1451  255
discrete levels.                                                  7128 1451  256
                                                                  7128 1451  257
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         7128 1451  258
                                                                  7128 1451  259
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  7128 1451  260
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   7128 1451  261
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      7128 1451  262
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  7128 1451  263
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      7128 1451  264
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         7128 1451  265
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   7128 1451  266
on an angular grid.                                               7128 1451  267
                                                                  7128 1451  268
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       7128 1451  269
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        7128 1451  270
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         7128 1451  271
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         7128 1451  272
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         7128 1451  273
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        7128 1451  274
                                                                  7128 1451  275
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     7128 1451  276
                                                                  7128 1451  277
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     7128 1451  278
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   7128 1451  279
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  7128 1451  280
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             7128 1451  281
All data are generated with TALYS.                                7128 1451  282
                                                                  7128 1451  283
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   7128 1451  284
                                                                  7128 1451  285
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      7128 1451  286
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   7128 1451  287
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   7128 1451  288
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are7128 1451  289
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   7128 1451  290
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  7128 1451  291
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              7128 1451  292
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 7128 1451  293
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  7128 1451  294
For each residual product:                                        7128 1451  295
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               7128 1451  296
- the recoil                                                      7128 1451  297
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         7128 1451  298
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     7128 1451  299
sections.                                                         7128 1451  300
                                                                  7128 1451  301
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     7128 1451  302
                                                                  7128 1451  303
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  7128 1451  304
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 7128 1451  305
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          7128 1451  306
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       7128 1451  307
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 7128 1451  308
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     7128 1451  309
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  7128 1451  310
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      7128 1451  311
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         7128 1451  312
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             7128 1451  313
                                                                  7128 1451  314
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   7128 1451  315
followed:                                                         7128 1451  316
                                                                  7128 1451  317
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         7128 1451  318
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         7128 1451  319
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         7128 1451  320
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     7128 1451  321
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     7128 1451  322
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     7128 1451  323
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     7128 1451  324
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     7128 1451  325
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     7128 1451  326
                                                                  7128 1451  327
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 7128 1451  328
                                                                  7128 1451  329
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    7128 1451  330
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          7128 1451  331
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     7128 1451  332
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          7128 1451  333
                                                                  7128 1451  334
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  7128 1451  335
                                                                  7128 1451  336
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     7128 1451  337
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    7128 1451  338
as isomer.                                                        7128 1451  339
                                                                  7128 1451  340
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 7128 1451  341
                                                                  7128 1451  342
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        7128 1451  343
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    7128 1451  344
as isomer.                                                        7128 1451  345
                                                                  7128 1451  346
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          7128 1451  347
                                                                  7128 1451  348
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:7128 1451  349
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   7128 1451  350
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   7128 1451  351
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   7128 1451  352
  given                                                           7128 1451  353
                                                                  7128 1451  354
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          7128 1451  355
                                                                  7128 1451  356
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  7128 1451  357
                                                                  7128 1451  358
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 7128 1451  359
                                                                  7128 1451  360
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         7128 1451  361
                                                                  7128 1451  362
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   7128 1451  363
                                                                  7128 1451  364
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        7128 1451  365
and more details are given above in this description. In most     7128 1451  366
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        7128 1451  367
                                                                  7128 1451  368
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 7128 1451  369
                                                                  7128 1451  370
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  7128 1451  371
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     7128 1451  372
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    7128 1451  373
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   7128 1451  374
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  7128 1451  375
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                7128 1451  376
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      7128 1451  377
   same reaction channel between different energies)              7128 1451  378
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      7128 1451  379
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               7128 1451  380
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      7128 1451  381
   different reaction channels for different energies)            7128 1451  382
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        7128 1451  383
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              7128 1451  384
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  7128 1451  385
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       7128 1451  386
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     7128 1451  387
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  7128 1451  388
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     7128 1451  389
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     7128 1451  390
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     7128 1451  391
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   7128 1451  392
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   7128 1451  393
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  7128 1451  394
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   7128 1451  395
                                                                  7128 1451  396
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  7128 1451  397
                                                                  7128 1451  398
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       7128 1451  399
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  7128 1451  400
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       7128 1451  401
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   7128 1451  402
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         7128 1451  403
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  7128 1451  404
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      7128 1451  405
representation is used.                                           7128 1451  406
                                                                  7128 1451  407
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      7128 1451  408
                                                                  7128 1451  409
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  7128 1451  410
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             7128 1451  411
